Arduino Coding
main() function is there, but it’s hidden under the covers by the Arduino build environment. The build
process creates an intermediate file that includes the sketch code and the followingadditional statements:
int main(void)
{
init();
setup();
for (;;)
loop();
return 0;
}
The first thing that happens is a call to an init() function that initializes the Arduino hardware. Next, your sketch’s setup() function
is called. Finally, your loop() function is called over and over. Because the for loop never terminates, the return statement is never executed.

